  "id": "Compound::DB00540 - Gene::146",
  "verbalization": "Nortriptyline, a tricyclic antidepressant, is shown to bind to Adra1a and Adra1b, which are both subtypes of the alpha-1 adrenergic receptor. These receptors are involved in the regulation of adrenergic signaling, and their participation in this activity is linked to Adra1d. The evidence suggests that Nortriptyline may modulate the activity of Adra1d through its interaction with Adra1a and Adra1b, which are functionally related. The scores from the paths indicate a consistent and significant interaction between Nortriptyline and Adra1d, suggesting a potential mechanism of action involving the alpha-1 adrenergic receptor. The overlapping roles of Adra1a and Adra1b in the same biological process support the idea that Nortriptyline may influence Adra1d through a shared pathway. The integration of these findings implies that Nortriptyline could exert its effects on Adra1d by targeting the alpha-1 adrenergic receptor, potentially through a mechanism involving receptor activation or modulation.",
